% 46-codegen-tests.weft — Code Generation Tests
%
% End-to-end tests for the ARM64 backend.  Each test is a small C
% program compiled by kcc, linked by the system linker, and executed.
% The test passes if the exit code matches the expected value.
% The shell script is tangled to tmp/tests/test_codegen.sh and run
% by 'just test-codegen'.

\chapter{Code Generation Tests}
\label{ch:codegen-tests}

The lexer conformance tests of Chapter~\ref{ch:conformance-tests} exercise
the compiler's frontend by calling internal functions from ARM64 assembly.
Code generation tests require a different strategy: they compile complete C
programs with \kcc{}, link the resulting object files into executables, and
verify that the process exits with the expected status code.  A shell script
orchestrates the pipeline---write source, compile, link, run, compare---and
reports results in the same pass/fail format as the unit test harness.

\section{Test Runner}

The script defines a helper function \texttt{run\_test} that receives a
test name, a C source string, and the expected exit code.  It writes the
source to a temporary file, invokes \texttt{tmp/kcc~-c} to produce an
object file, links it with the system linker, runs the resulting binary,
and compares the actual exit code against the expectation.  Failures print
the expected and actual values for diagnosis.

@d Test: codegen runner @{
#!/bin/bash
# kcc code generation test suite
# Generated by weft — do not edit
set -u

PASS=0
FAIL=0
TOTAL=0
KCC="tmp/kcc"
TMPDIR="/tmp/kcc_test_$$"
SDK=$(xcrun --show-sdk-path 2>/dev/null)

mkdir -p "$TMPDIR"
trap 'rm -rf "$TMPDIR"' EXIT

run_test() {
    local name="$1"
    local src="$2"
    local expected="$3"
    TOTAL=$((TOTAL + 1))

    echo "$src" > "$TMPDIR/t.c"

    # Compile
    if ! $KCC -c "$TMPDIR/t.c" -o "$TMPDIR/t.o" 2>/dev/null; then
        FAIL=$((FAIL + 1))
        printf "  %-40s FAIL (compile error)\n" "$name..."
        return
    fi

    # Link
    if ! ld "$TMPDIR/t.o" -o "$TMPDIR/t" \
            -lSystem -syslibroot "$SDK" \
            -e _main -arch arm64 2>/dev/null; then
        FAIL=$((FAIL + 1))
        printf "  %-40s FAIL (link error)\n" "$name..."
        return
    fi

    # Run
    "$TMPDIR/t"
    local actual=$?

    if [ "$actual" -eq "$expected" ]; then
        PASS=$((PASS + 1))
        printf "  %-40s PASS\n" "$name..."
    else
        FAIL=$((FAIL + 1))
        printf "  %-40s FAIL (expected %d, got %d)\n" "$name..." "$expected" "$actual"
    fi
}

echo "kcc codegen test suite"
echo "======================"
@}

\section{Constants and Arithmetic}

The most basic backend test: a function that returns a constant,
verifying that \texttt{IR\_CONST} and \texttt{IR\_RET} lower
correctly.  Then arithmetic operators confirm that the scratch-register
shuttle (\texttt{x9}/\texttt{x10}/\texttt{x11}) computes correct
results.

@d Test: codegen constants @{
echo ""
echo "--- Constants and Arithmetic ---"

run_test "return constant" \
    "int main(void) { return 42; }" 42

run_test "addition" \
    "int main(void) { return 2 + 3; }" 5

run_test "subtraction" \
    "int main(void) { return 10 - 3; }" 7

run_test "multiplication" \
    "int main(void) { return 6 * 7; }" 42

run_test "division" \
    "int main(void) { return 100 / 10; }" 10

run_test "modulo" \
    "int main(void) { return 17 % 5; }" 2

run_test "complex arithmetic" \
    "int main(void) { return 2 + 3 * 4; }" 14

run_test "negation" \
    "int main(void) { return -(-42); }" 42

run_test "bitwise and" \
    "int main(void) { return 0xFF & 0x0F; }" 15

run_test "bitwise or" \
    "int main(void) { return 0xF0 | 0x0F; }" 255

run_test "bitwise xor" \
    "int main(void) { return 0xFF ^ 0x0F; }" 240

run_test "left shift" \
    "int main(void) { return 1 << 4; }" 16

run_test "right shift" \
    "int main(void) { return 32 >> 2; }" 8
@}

\section{Local Variables}

Variable declarations allocate stack slots via \texttt{IR\_ALLOCA}.
These tests verify that values stored in locals survive across
statements and that multiple locals occupy distinct slots.

@d Test: codegen locals @{
echo ""
echo "--- Local Variables ---"

run_test "local variable" \
    "int main(void) { int x = 10; return x; }" 10

run_test "two locals" \
    "int main(void) { int a = 3; int b = 4; return a + b; }" 7

run_test "local reassignment" \
    "int main(void) { int x = 1; x = 42; return x; }" 42

run_test "char literal" \
    "int main(void) { char c = 'A'; return c; }" 65
@}

\section{Control Flow}

Control flow tests exercise \texttt{IR\_BR\_COND} and
\texttt{IR\_BR}, including forward branches (if/else) and
back-edges (loops).  The for-loop test uses the explicit
\texttt{i = i + 1} form to isolate loop lowering from
increment operators.

@d Test: codegen control flow @{
echo ""
echo "--- Control Flow ---"

run_test "if true" \
    "int main(void) { if (1) return 10; return 20; }" 10

run_test "if false" \
    "int main(void) { if (0) return 10; return 20; }" 20

run_test "if-else" \
    "int main(void) { int x = 5; if (x > 3) return 1; else return 0; }" 1

run_test "nested if" \
    "int main(void) { int x = 5; if (x > 0) { if (x > 3) return 1; return 2; } return 3; }" 1

run_test "while loop" \
    "int main(void) { int x = 0; while (x < 10) x = x + 1; return x; }" 10

run_test "do-while loop" \
    "int main(void) { int i = 0; int s = 0; do { s = s + i; i = i + 1; } while (i <= 5); return s; }" 15

run_test "for loop" \
    "int main(void) { int s = 0; for (int i = 0; i < 5; i = i + 1) s = s + 2; return s; }" 10

run_test "break" \
    "int main(void) { int i = 0; while (1) { if (i == 5) break; i = i + 1; } return i; }" 5

run_test "continue" \
    "int main(void) { int s = 0; for (int i = 0; i < 10; i = i + 1) { if (i % 2 == 0) continue; s = s + 1; } return s; }" 5

run_test "multiple returns" \
    "int f(int x) { if (x > 0) return 1; if (x < 0) return 2; return 0; } int main(void) { return f(5) + f(-3) + f(0); }" 3
@}

\section{Increment and Decrement}

These tests exercise the load-add/sub-store lowering of the
\texttt{++} and \texttt{-{}-} operators added in
Chapter~\ref{ch:ir-builder}.  The postfix form returns the value
\emph{before} modification; the prefix form returns the value
\emph{after}.  The for-loop test with \texttt{i++} is the most
critical: before this feature was implemented, the loop ran
forever because the increment emitted \texttt{IR\_NOP}.

@d Test: codegen increment @{
echo ""
echo "--- Increment / Decrement ---"

run_test "postfix increment stmt" \
    "int main(void) { int x = 5; x++; return x; }" 6

run_test "prefix increment" \
    "int main(void) { int x = 5; return ++x; }" 6

run_test "postfix returns old" \
    "int main(void) { int x = 5; int y = x++; return y; }" 5

run_test "prefix returns new" \
    "int main(void) { int x = 5; int y = ++x; return y; }" 6

run_test "postfix decrement stmt" \
    "int main(void) { int x = 5; x--; return x; }" 4

run_test "prefix decrement" \
    "int main(void) { int x = 5; return --x; }" 4

run_test "for-loop with i++" \
    "int main(void) { int s = 0; for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) s = s + 2; return s; }" 10

run_test "for-loop sum with i++" \
    "int main(void) { int s = 0; for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) s = s + i; return s; }" 10

run_test "do-while with i++" \
    "int main(void) { int i = 0; int s = 0; do { s = s + i; i++; } while (i <= 5); return s; }" 15

run_test "while with decrement" \
    "int main(void) { int x = 5; while (x > 0) x--; return x; }" 0

run_test "triple increment" \
    "int main(void) { int x = 0; x++; x++; x++; return x; }" 3
@}

\section{Logical and Comparison Operators}

Short-circuit evaluation for \texttt{\&\&} and \texttt{||} produces
conditional branches; these tests verify both the truthiness rules
and the fact that side effects on the right-hand side are correctly
skipped when the left-hand side determines the result.

@d Test: codegen logical @{
echo ""
echo "--- Logical and Comparison ---"

run_test "logical and true" \
    "int main(void) { return 1 && 1; }" 1

run_test "logical and false" \
    "int main(void) { return 1 && 0; }" 0

run_test "logical or" \
    "int main(void) { return 0 || 1; }" 1

run_test "logical not" \
    "int main(void) { return !0; }" 1

run_test "logical not true" \
    "int main(void) { return !42; }" 0

run_test "chained comparison" \
    "int main(void) { return (3 > 2) + (2 > 1); }" 2

run_test "equality" \
    "int main(void) { return (5 == 5) + (5 != 3); }" 2

run_test "less/greater" \
    "int main(void) { return (1 < 2) + (2 > 1) + (2 <= 2) + (3 >= 3); }" 4

run_test "complex logical" \
    "int main(void) { return (1 && 0) || (1 && 1); }" 1
@}

\section{Functions}

Function calls marshal arguments through \texttt{x0}--\texttt{x7}
and recover the return value from \texttt{x0}.  These tests cover
single-argument, multi-argument, and nested calls, as well as
locally-defined helper functions.

@d Test: codegen functions @{
echo ""
echo "--- Functions ---"

run_test "simple call" \
    "int foo(int x) { return x + 1; } int main(void) { return foo(41); }" 42

run_test "nested calls" \
    "int foo(int x) { return x + 1; } int main(void) { return foo(foo(1)); }" 3

run_test "multi-arg" \
    "int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; } int main(void) { return add(20, 22); }" 42

run_test "three args" \
    "int sum3(int a, int b, int c) { return a + b + c; } int main(void) { return sum3(10, 20, 12); }" 42

run_test "recursive factorial" \
    "int fact(int n) { if (n <= 1) return 1; return n * fact(n - 1); } int main(void) { return fact(5); }" 120
@}

\section{Pointers}

Pointer tests exercise \texttt{IR\_ALLOCA} (address-of), \texttt{IR\_LOAD}
(dereference for reading), and \texttt{IR\_STORE} (dereference for writing).

@d Test: codegen pointers @{
echo ""
echo "--- Pointers ---"

run_test "address and deref" \
    "int main(void) { int x = 42; int *p = &x; return *p; }" 42

run_test "cast expression" \
    "int main(void) { long x = 42; return (int)x; }" 42
@}

\section{Miscellaneous}

A catch-all section for operators and constructs that do not fit
neatly into the categories above: the comma operator, the
\texttt{return~0} idiom, and zero-value edge cases.

@d Test: codegen misc @{
echo ""
echo "--- Miscellaneous ---"

run_test "comma operator" \
    "int main(void) { return (1, 2, 3); }" 3

run_test "return zero" \
    "int main(void) { return 0; }" 0

run_test "large constant" \
    "int main(void) { return 255; }" 255
@}

\section{Results}

After all test groups have run, the script prints a summary line
and exits with status~0 only if every test passed.

@d Test: codegen results @{
echo ""
echo "======================"
echo "Results: $PASS passed, $FAIL failed (of $TOTAL)"
[ "$FAIL" -eq 0 ]
@}
